  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and some embedded \nthunderstorms expected today. Isolated instances of flooding \npossible at the coast and isolated severe weather possible for \nwestern counties. \n\nA quasi-stationary boundary with weak low pressure along it is still \nlocated just south of the area as of mid-day surface analysis. A \ncold front is approaching the Appalachian region. Aloft, a trough \naxis is located just to the west, placing the FA under SW flow. \nRadar trends have been interesting today. Due to conflicting flows \nat the surface and in the mid/upper levels, showers have been nearly \nstationary. Luckily, these have been mostly limited to over the \nmarine areas, so flood advisories and warnings have not been \nnecessary so far. There is also enough shear to support \nrotation in one of the storms over the Ches Bay, which resulted \nin an SMW. Additional isolated showers are starting to pop up \nover coastal land areas, though. With little to no movement, \nPWATs climbing toward 2\", and instability sitting at ~1000 J/kg,\nflash flooding could still be a threat where these showers pop \nup. Based on the HREF/REFS outputs, the highest threat may be on\nthe Eastern Shore once the sfc front slides northeast and those\nexisting showers finally drift east. This thinking lines up \nwell with the updated Day 1 ERO that trimmed the Slight Risk \n(level 2/4) to just the Eastern Shore. These showers (and a few \nrumbles of thunder) will likely persist into this evening until \nthe incoming cold front kicks them out. \n\nSpeaking of the cold front, it will likely sweep through the local \narea during the overnight hours. Additional showers/storms will be \npossible with the front, particularly across the piedmont and \nnorthern counties. The 12z HREF indicates that 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE \nalong with 25-30kt of shear should linger long enough into the \nevening to support a couple of stronger storms. The SPC did expand \nthe Marginal risk for severe weather into the western counties to \ncover this. Damaging wind gusts is the primary threat. Timing looks \nto be 8p-12a. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures Sunday \nthrough Tuesday. Isolated to scattered diurnally driven convection \nreturns by the middle to latter part of the week.\n\nThe weak front will finally move offshore late Sunday, as the main \nupper level trough axis shifts offshore and surface high pressure \nbuilds into the SE CONUS. The surface high will usher in a drier \nairmass with seasonable temperatures in the 80s and dew points in \nthe 60s Sunday. A reinforcing intrusion of dry air is likely Monday/ \nTuesday with dew points in the 50s looking likely (except 60s far \nSE). Unsettled conditions and isolated- scattered aftn/evening \nconvection returning mid to late week as the sfc high breaks\ndown.",
